nfectious SARS Cop 2 in the feces and  So currently there's no real clear data  that has shown that you can actually get  an infectious virus other than just  genome and there's still studies going  on trying to understand if the virus  itself is infectious or if it's just  shedding genome um at this time  and so the work in the lab that we've  been doing in the model system we're  using our in human mini gut organized so  for us we derive our organoids from  patients biopsies or resections so we  get patient derived material from this  we isolate the stem cell Crypt this  Crypt is then seated into Machu gel and  given differentiation factors and then  over several days it's allowed to seal  and reform and what you can see in this  uh black and white image here in the  center is an actual what an actual  organoid would look like with a light  shell a light sheet microscope and the  reason we like to use this as our model  is because these cells are primary and  non-transformed so we don't have to  worry about having any sort of cancer  phenotypes interfering with our readouts  they can recapitulate the cellular  complexity of the intestinal epithelium  so these are cells are not just made up  of one cell type but they're multiple  cell types they have the structural  Arrangements like we see in the  intestine they also have mucus and other  components that give it a much more in  viewable feel than a normal cell culture  they can also be genetically modifiable  so we can knock out  reagents in it we can also over Express  things in them and they can be patient  specific so we can also look at specific  patients or people that have a certain  condition to see if they're more  susceptible to Virus Infection as well  and so the first train that I've been  using for all the studies are the bath  path too so this was an original isolate  from February of 2020 the wood we took  um neural nasal orophone geoswabs from  infected patients and we isolated the  virus on Vero ce