ections so we know that our  organoids are containing our cell types  and that the SARS infection is not  altering the cell type distribution  so we then can go back and look at our  umaps and then plot on these umaps where  SARS Cop 2 is present so you all the  little colored triangles are the cells  that are expressing SARS [ __ ] 2 genome  and so the more yellow the more genome  they're expressing and what you can  notice is that these are in discrete  populations so we're not seeing all cell  types expressing virus genome but we're  seeing them in very small distributions  indicating that this is a more limited  viral tropism  and so if we analyze this and look at  the cell types that is expressing it  what we can see is that we get an  increase in virus infection from 12 to  24 hours but we also see that we have a  main population which is the immature  enterocytes 2 which are supporting the  most infection at both time points so we  can say that we have a restricted  tropism and that SARS cox2  preferentially infects human immature  enterocytes and I just want to point out  here at the top where we have  enterocytes one that we show very very  little infection and this will be  important in the next slide  and so what we wanted to then do is also  to go back and look at the proposed  entry factors and look at how they were  distributed all over these different  cell type populations so here on the  left we have all the different potential  SARS Cop 2 entry factors and on the  bottom we have all of our different cell  type populations and what I showed you  in the previous slide is that immature  enterocyte two were the ones that  supported SARS Cox II infection the best  and the interesting thing that we can  see about this is that H2 expression  which is known to be a key receptor for  SARS Cop 2 is not the thing that will  actually tell us which would be the most  infected cells so if we actually looked  at our enterocyte ones these are the  ones that are di